# THE CRLF CURE — cut by BIP 2BBIP11, 2026-09-19, off WSS-07's finding.
#
# WSS-07 spent a round restoring three pages that had been rewritten end to end
# with no content change at all. The culprit outlived the round: core.autocrlf
# is set to true MACHINE-WIDE on this PC, and `git status` will never show you
# that. Every coder that checks this repo out rewrites LF files to CRLF, and the
# whole file then reads as modified. It has cost the floor hours twice and it
# WILL recur, because nothing in the repo contradicted the machine setting.
#
# .gitattributes is the only cure that survives a fresh clone, a second machine
# and whatever a future agent's global config happens to say: it overrides
# core.autocrlf per-repository. The repo stores LF; the worktree gets LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one place CRLF is correct. cmd.exe and PowerShell both want it, and a .cmd
# checked out with LF can misparse a multi-line block. These are checked out CRLF
# and stored LF, so they stop showing up as phantom modifications either way.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Binary. text=auto already detects these; naming them makes it unambiguous and
# stops any future global setting from touching an image or a font.
*.png   -text
*.jpg   -text
*.jpeg  -text
*.gif   -text
*.ico   -text
*.webp  -text
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.woff  -text
*.woff2 -text
*.ttf   -text
*.pdf   -text
*.mp4   -text
*.zip   -text
